  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nLow level moisture in place will continue to result in areas of\nfog and stratus for areas south of Pittsburgh early this\nmorning. This should mix out and dissipate after sunrise.\nOtherwise, dry weather is expected today for most of the area as\nhigh pressure begins to build in behind a cold front to our\nsouth. Isolated showers or possibly a thunderstorm are possible\nacross the far southern portion of the forecast area (south of a\nZZV-MGW line) as an inverted surface trough briefly drifts north\ntoward the area. Highs should be near seasonable levels.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModel ensembles indicate a strong ridge will build toward the\nUpper Ohio Valley region through the middle of the upcoming\nweek. Increasing temperatures are expected with riding 500 mb\nheights. Wednesday should be the warmest day with 500 mb heights\napproaching 594 dm. Highs by then should be in the lower 90s\noutside of the higher terrain, with heat index values generally\nin the mid 90s. \n\nThe ridge is progged to begin to break down later in the week,\nwith a slight decrease in temperatures. NW flow aloft is then\nexpected to develop into next weekend, with possible crossing\nshortwaves and convection potential increasing.",
